titre$ G Y M N O P I L U S   -   P.D. Orton in British Fungus Flora 7, 1993
com$ Adaptée par Paul Bertéa 13-09-2011

1,2 $ On stumps, trunks and other woody debris or if apparently on the ground, then attached to buried wood
1,9 $ On soil or burnt sites or on grass-tufts (Dactylis glomerata) $ Section Gymnopilus $s"Section Gymnopilus"

2,0 $ Stem with distinct membranous ring; usually robust and often caespitose; spores (7.5-)8-10/5-6 µm; on deciduous wood $ G. junonius $s"Section Juoninus"
2,3 $ Stem without membranous ring but frequently with an apical ring-zone of fibrils from veil and with velar fragments on the cap margin, more rarely pruinose or without veil $ Section Gymnopilus $s"Section Gymnopilus"

3,0 $ Spores small, 3.5-4.5(-5.7) / 2.5-3.5 µm; (cap small, 7-25 mm. and markedly reddish in colour; on coniferous wood) $ G. bellulus 
3,4 $ Spores clearly larger [3.5-4.5(-5.7) / 2.5-3.5 µm]

4,5 $ Stem with cortinate veil traces at first or ± silky-fibrillose-striate; spores 6-9/4-5 µm.
4,8 $ Stem pruinose when young then sometimes at apex only; veIl absent or very slight; spores (7.5-)8-10/5-6 µm

5,6 $ Cap ± entirely silky or fibrillose tomentose or scaly at first; on coniferous wood
5,7 $ Cap ± smooth from the first; on coniferous or deciduous wood

6,0 $ Cap unusually thick and fleshy, saffron or fulvous then sienna, cinnamon or rusty-tawny, sometimes yellowish-ochre (G) around margin, ± entirely adpressed silky or fibrillose tomentose at first; stem yellowish-cream (F) or ochre (G) $ G. stabilis
6,0 $ Cap not so fleshy, rusty-tawny or dark-brick sometimes with ± orange margin, ± entirely finely and slightly reflexed scaly; stem yellowish-cream (C, E) then fulvous, sienna, cinnamon or rusty-tawny from base up $ G. sapineus

7,0 $ Gills soon spotted sienna or sienna-cinnamon; veil lacking or very slight; cap not or only slightly tinged apricot or orangeb $ G. penetrans
7,0 $ Gills not becoming spotted although the edge may become ± sienna with age; cortinate veil forming apical but often fugacious ring-zone when fresh; cap sometimes with apricot or orange tinge $ G. hybridus

8,0 $ Stem umber at least in lower part $ G. picreus
8,0 $ Stem orange, sienna, rust or rusty-tawny $ G. liquiritiae

9,0 $ On cock's-foot grass-tufts (Dactylis glomerata); spores 4.5-5.5/3.5-4 µm $ G. flavus
9,10 $ On soil or burnt ground or wood; spores clearly larger

10,0 $ Spores 8-10(-1l)/(5-)6-7 µm; on heaths or sand-dunes amongst moss or lichens or on peaty soil (rarely on burnt soil) $ G. fulgens
10,11 $ Spores smaller and narrower within the range of 6-9/3.4-5 µm; usually on burnt soil or wood


11,0 $ Smell and taste ± mealy $ G. odini
11,0,N$///Cap smooth or breaking up into small scales; cap and stem umber at least in part; spores 6-8/3.4-4.5 µm; marginal cystidia [sec. Romagnesi] narrow and inconspicuous
11,0 $ Smell and taste not distinct $ G. decipiens
11,0,N$///Cap minutely floccose-scaly or tomentose; cap brightly coloured, not umber; stem sienna or rusty-tawny more rarely umber in lower part; spores 7-9/4-5 µm; marginal cystidia well-marked, lageniform capitate

